    (mid, $S$Choosing Your Accounting Method$S$, $S$choosing-your-accounting-method$S$, $C$<h3><strong>1. Choosing an Accounting Method</strong></h3><p><strong>Cash Basis</strong></p><ul><li>Record income when money hits the bank.</li><li>Record expenses when you actually pay them.</li><li>Simple, easy to manage.</li><li>Often preferred by small businesses, freelancers, service-based companies.</li><li>Taxes are based on actual cash in/out.</li></ul><p><strong>Accrual Basis</strong></p><ul><li>Record income when it’s earned (invoice date), even if unpaid.</li><li>Record expenses when they’re incurred, even if unpaid.</li><li>Required by IRS if revenue &gt; $25M.</li><li>Preferred by investors, lenders, and product-based businesses.</li><li>Gives a clearer picture of profitability and obligations.</li></ul><p><strong>Best Practices</strong></p><ul><li>Start with <strong>cash basis</strong> if you’re under $1M revenue and don’t carry inventory.</li><li>Use <strong>accrual basis</strong> if you:<ul><li>Plan to seek outside investment/loans soon</li><li>Carry inventory</li><li>Want more accurate monthly reporting</li></ul></li></ul><p>✅ <strong>Action Step</strong>: Decide and document your chosen method in your Financial SOPs.<br /> Most accounting software lets you toggle reporting between cash and accrual, but you need to pick one as your <em>official tax method</em>.</p>$C$, 1, true),
    (mid, $S$Creating Your Chart of Accounts$S$, $S$creating-your-chart-of-accounts$S$, $C$<p><strong>What is a Chart of Accounts?</strong></p><p>A Chart of Accounts (COA) is like the master list of categories your business uses to track money. Every time money comes in or goes out, it gets assigned to one of these categories so you can see where your cash is really going.</p><p>Think of it like the folder system on a computer:</p><p>Each folder has a name (like “Sales” or “Rent”) and a number code.</p><p>When you save a file (transaction), you drop it in the right folder.</p><p>This keeps your books organized, so you don’t have to guess at tax time or when applying for a loan.</p><p><strong>Why It Matters</strong></p><p>Helps you understand profits vs. expenses clearly.</p><p>Makes tax time simple (your CPA already knows the categories).</p><p>Builds credibility with banks and investors (clean, professional reporting).</p><p>Prevents messy books (no personal and business money mixed together).</p><p><strong>Example (Small Business COA in action)</strong></p><p>Sales Revenue (4010): Customer pays $1,000 → goes here.</p><p>Marketing Expense (6020): You spend $200 on ads → goes here.</p><p>Equipment (1300): You buy a $500 laptop → goes here.</p><p>At the end of the month, you can instantly see:</p><p>How much money came in (revenue).</p><p>What you spent to deliver your product/service (COGS).</p><p>What you spent to run the business (expenses).</p><p>What’s left over (profit).</p><p>✅ In short:<br /> A Chart of Accounts is the blueprint of your bookkeeping system — it organizes every dollar so you can make smart financial decisions.</p><p><strong>Chart of Accounts</strong></p><p>Purpose</p><p>To create a structured, consistent system for categorizing income, expenses, assets, liabilities, and equity across the business.</p><p>Core COA Categories (standard for all businesses)</p><p>1xxx – Assets</p><p>1010 Cash – Operating Account</p><p>1020 Cash – Savings / Reserves</p><p>1030 Accounts Receivable</p><p>1200 Inventory (if applicable)</p><p>1300 Equipment &amp; Furniture</p><p>1400 Prepaid Expenses</p><p>2xxx – Liabilities</p><p>2010 Accounts Payable</p><p>2020 Credit Card Payable</p><p>2100 Payroll Liabilities</p><p>2200 Sales Tax Payable</p><p>2300 Loans Payable</p><p>3xxx – Equity</p><p>3100 Owner’s Capital / Common Stock</p><p>3200 Retained Earnings</p><p>3300 Owner’s Draws / Distributions</p><p>4xxx – Revenue</p><p>4010 Product Sales</p><p>4020 Service Revenue</p><p>4030 Subscription/Recurring Revenue</p><p>4040 Discounts/Refunds (contra-revenue)</p><p>5xxx – Cost of Goods Sold (COGS)</p><p>5010 Direct Materials &amp; Supplies</p><p>5020 Direct Labor</p><p>5030 Subcontractors</p><p>5040 Shipping/Inbound Freight</p><p>6xxx – Operating Expenses</p><p>6010 Salaries &amp; Wages – Admin</p><p>6020 Marketing &amp; Advertising</p><p>6030 Rent / Lease</p><p>6040 Utilities</p><p>6050 Software &amp; Subscriptions</p><p>6060 Insurance</p><p>6070 Professional Fees (CPA, Legal)</p><p>6080 Travel &amp; Meals</p><p>6090 Training &amp; Education</p><p>7xxx – Other Income</p><p>7010 Interest Income</p><p>7020 Grant Income</p><p>7030 Miscellaneous Income</p><p>8xxx – Other Expenses</p><p>8010 Interest Expense</p><p>8020 Penalties/Fines</p><p>9xxx – Taxes</p><p>9010 Income Tax Expense</p><p>9020 Franchise/Entity Tax</p><p>Best Practices for Chart of Accounts</p><p>Keep it lean → Don’t create a code for every single thing; group logically.</p><p>Future-proof → Leave gaps in numbering (e.g., 6010, 6020, 6030…) so you can add more later.</p><p>Industry-specific add-ons →</p><p>Construction: Retainage Receivable/Payable, Job Costs.</p><p>SaaS: Hosting fees, API usage, Customer Success.</p><p>Nonprofit: Donor-restricted vs. unrestricted revenue.</p><p>COGS vs. OpEx → Only direct costs tied to sales go in COGS; everything else is OpEx.</p><p>Consistency matters → Once a category is chosen, don’t move transactions around.</p><p>Decision Guide: Which COA to Use</p><p>Freelancer/Solo (&lt;$100K) → Keep it ultra-simple: cash, payables, 1–2 revenue codes, 5–10 expense codes.</p><p>Growing Small Biz ($100K–$1M) → Use full structure above with separate COGS and OpEx.</p><p>Inventory/Construction → Add inventory, job costs, and retainage accounts.</p><p>Nonprofit → Split revenue by restricted vs unrestricted and track net assets accordingly.</p><p>Scaling ($1M+) → Add departments/classes inside your COA for deeper reporting (e.g., Marketing, Operations, Sales).</p><p>✅ Action Step: Select or customize the COA that fits your industry, load it into your accounting software, and lock it down as your default framework.</p>$C$, 2, true),
    (mid, $S$Software & Tools Selection$S$, $S$software-tools-selection$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Purpose</strong></h3><p>Choose the right system to manage your books, automate as much as possible, and keep your financials clean and fundable.</p><h2><strong>Core Software Options</strong></h2><h3><strong>1. QuickBooks Online (QBO)</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Best for:</strong> Most small–mid businesses; standard for CPAs and lenders.</li><li><strong>Pros:</strong><ul><li>Widely accepted (banks, investors, and accountants trust QBO data).</li><li>Tons of integrations (banks, payroll, e-commerce, CRMs).</li><li>Scales well (add users, reporting, classes/departments).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Cons:</strong> Monthly subscription; learning curve for beginners.</li><li><strong>Best practice:</strong> Use QBO if you plan on fundraising, scaling beyond $500K, or needing advanced reporting.</li></ul><h3><strong>2. Xero</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Best for:</strong> Global or online-first companies; SaaS/startups.</li><li><strong>Pros:</strong><ul><li>Cloud-native, sleek UI.</li><li>Strong multi-currency support.</li><li>Open API for automation.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Cons:</strong> Less common in U.S. (some CPAs prefer QBO).</li><li><strong>Best practice:</strong> Use Xero if you run SaaS, have international operations, or want better integrations for tech stacks.</li></ul><h3><strong>3. Wave</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Best for:</strong> Solopreneurs, freelancers, early-stage small businesses.</li><li><strong>Pros:</strong><ul><li>Free core bookkeeping.</li><li>Simple invoicing and payment processing.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Cons:</strong> Limited scalability; weak reporting; not lender-preferred.</li><li><strong>Best practice:</strong> Use Wave if your business is &lt;$100K revenue and you need a free option until you grow.</li></ul><h3><strong>4. FreshBooks</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Best for:</strong> Service-based businesses and consultants.</li><li><strong>Pros:</strong><ul><li>Clean invoicing and time-tracking.</li><li>Good for freelancers who bill clients directly.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Cons:</strong> Limited advanced accounting features.</li><li><strong>Best practice:</strong> Use FreshBooks if your primary need is sending invoices, tracking time, and getting paid faster.</li></ul><h3><strong>5. Zoho Books</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Best for:</strong> Businesses already using Zoho ecosystem.</li><li><strong>Pros:</strong> Affordable; integrates seamlessly with Zoho CRM, Inventory, etc.</li><li><strong>Cons:</strong> Not as widely accepted by accountants.</li><li><strong>Best practice:</strong> Use Zoho if you’re already running Zoho CRM/Projects and want one ecosystem.</li></ul><h3><strong>6. Specialized Add-Ons</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Gusto, ADP, or Paychex</strong> → Payroll &amp; HR compliance.</li><li><strong>Bill.com or Melio</strong> → Automated payables/receivables.</li><li><strong>Expensify or Divvy</strong> → Employee expense management.</li><li><strong>Hubdoc / Dext</strong> → Automated receipt scanning into accounting software.</li></ul><h2><strong>Decision Guide by Scenario</strong></h2><table><tr><td><p><strong>Scenario</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>Best Software</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>Why</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Solo entrepreneur &lt;$100K</strong></p></td><td><p>Wave (free) → upgrade to QBO later</p></td><td><p>No cost, simple until you grow.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Service-based small biz $100K–$500K</strong></p></td><td><p>FreshBooks or QBO Simple Start</p></td><td><p>Strong invoicing; QBO if scaling.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Product-based biz w/ inventory</strong></p></td><td><p>QuickBooks Online + SOS Inventory</p></td><td><p>Handles COGS, stock, reorders.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>SaaS / Online business</strong></p></td><td><p>Xero (multi-currency, API friendly)</p></td><td><p>Flexible for subscription revenue.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Nonprofit</strong></p></td><td><p>QBO Nonprofit edition or Aplos</p></td><td><p>Fund accounting, donor tracking.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Scaling beyond $500K or fundraising</strong></p></td><td><p>QuickBooks Online</p></td><td><p>Lender- and investor-preferred.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>International operations</strong></p></td><td><p>Xero</p></td><td><p>Strong global compliance &amp; multi-currency.</p></td></tr></table><h2><strong>Best Practices for Software Selection</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Future-proof:</strong> Choose a system that can scale 3–5 years ahead.</li><li><strong>Integrations first:</strong> Pick software that integrates with your CRM, bank, payroll, or POS.</li><li><strong>CPA alignment:</strong> Confirm your accountant is fluent in the software you select.</li><li><strong>Automation:</strong> Prioritize platforms that auto-import bank/credit transactions daily.</li><li><strong>Cloud-first:</strong> Always choose cloud-based systems for access and backups.</li></ul><p>✅ <strong>Action Step:</strong> Decide your core accounting platform now based on your current size and 2–3 year growth plans. Document it in your <strong>Financial SOP</strong> so you don’t bounce between systems later.</p>$C$, 3, true),
    (mid, $S$Revenue Models & Pricing$S$, $S$revenue-models-pricing$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Purpose</strong></h3><p>To define how the business earns money, establish clear pricing strategies, and ensure margins are sustainable.</p><h2><strong>1. Common Revenue Models</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>One-Time Sales</strong> – Sell a product/service once (retail, project work).</li><li><strong>Recurring / Subscription</strong> – Customers pay monthly or annually (SaaS, memberships).</li><li><strong>Freemium / Tiered</strong> – Free basic access; paid for premium features.</li><li><strong>Pay-Per-Use</strong> – Customer pays based on actual usage (utilities, cloud services).</li><li><strong>Licensing / Royalties</strong> – Allow others to use your IP or products for a fee.</li><li><strong>Hybrid Models</strong> – Mix of recurring and one-time sales (e.g., gym membership + personal training).</li></ul><h2><strong>2. Pricing Strategies</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Cost-Plus Pricing</strong><ul><li>Add markup to your costs.</li><li>Example: If service costs $50, add 100% markup → $100 price.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Value-Based Pricing</strong><ul><li>Price based on customer perception of value, not just cost.</li><li>Example: Customer saves $10K, you charge $2K.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Market-Based Pricing</strong><ul><li>Price in line with competitors (above, at, or below).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Penetration Pricing</strong><ul><li>Start low to gain market share, raise later.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Premium Pricing</strong><ul><li>Higher than average → positioned as higher quality.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Dynamic Pricing</strong><ul><li>Adjust based on demand, season, or availability (hotels, airlines).</li></ul></li></ul><h2><strong>3. Pricing Best Practices</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Know Your Numbers:</strong><ul><li>Calculate <em>Cost of Goods Sold (COGS)</em> + <em>Operating Expenses (OpEx)</em>.</li><li>Ensure prices cover costs <strong>plus profit margin</strong> (minimum 20–30%).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Test &amp; Adjust:</strong><ul><li>Run pricing experiments; don’t lock in too early.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Segment Customers:</strong><ul><li>Create pricing tiers for different buyer groups.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Psychological Pricing:</strong><ul><li>Use $99 instead of $100; bundle items for perceived savings.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Review Regularly:</strong><ul><li>Review pricing quarterly against costs and competitors.</li></ul></li></ul><h2><strong>4. Revenue Planning Worksheet (Framework)</strong></h2><ol><li>List all revenue streams (products, services, memberships, etc.).</li><li>Identify pricing model (subscription, one-time, etc.).</li><li>Set price point(s).</li><li>Forecast sales volume (monthly/annual).</li><li>Calculate expected revenue = Price × Volume.</li><li>Subtract direct costs (COGS) → Gross Profit.</li><li>Subtract operating expenses → Net Profit.</li></ol><h2><strong>5. Industry-Specific Examples</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Construction/Trades</strong> → Project bids, change orders, cost-plus contracts.</li><li><strong>SaaS</strong> → Subscription tiers (basic, pro, enterprise).</li><li><strong>Retail</strong> → One-time product sales + loyalty programs.</li><li><strong>Consulting/Coaching</strong> → Hourly rate, retainer, or packages.</li><li><strong>Nonprofit</strong> → Program fees + grants + donations.</li></ul><p>✅ <strong>Action Step:</strong> Select your revenue model(s), run through the worksheet, and confirm your pricing covers COGS, OpEx, and desired profit margins.</p><div class="lesson-template"><h3>📄 Template for this lesson</h3><p>Download and use the worksheet that goes with this lesson:</p><a class="dl" href="/templates/financial-advantage/pricing-strategy-worksheet.docx" download="Pricing Strategy Worksheet.docx">⬇ Download Pricing Strategy Worksheet</a></div>$C$, 4, true),
    (mid, $S$Payroll Setup$S$, $S$payroll-setup$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Purpose</strong></h3><p>Payroll is a high-risk area for small businesses. Done right, it keeps you compliant, protects you from IRS penalties, and makes sure employees (and contractors) are paid on time. Done wrong, it can trigger fines, back taxes, and even personal liability.</p><p>To establish a compliant, efficient payroll system that properly classifies workers, handles taxes, and integrates with bookkeeping.</p><h2><strong>1. Worker Classification</strong></h2><p>☐ <strong>W-2 Employees</strong> – Regular staff; employer must withhold taxes, pay employer taxes, provide W-2 at year-end.<br /> ☐ <strong>1099 Contractors</strong> – Independent workers; paid gross, responsible for their own taxes; issue 1099-NEC if &gt;$600 in a year.<br /> ⚠️ <strong>Best practice:</strong> Always classify correctly. Misclassification = IRS penalties + back payroll taxes.</p><h2><strong>2. Choose Payroll System</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Gusto</strong> → Best all-around for small businesses; automates tax filings.</li><li><strong>ADP</strong> → Good for larger teams; strong HR compliance.</li><li><strong>Paychex</strong> → Strong reporting and benefits options.</li><li><strong>QuickBooks Payroll</strong> → Seamless if already using QuickBooks.</li><li><strong>Square Payroll</strong> → Simple if you already use Square for payments.</li></ul><p><strong>Decision rule:</strong></p><ul><li>&lt;10 employees → Gusto or Square Payroll.</li><li>10–50 employees → Gusto or QuickBooks Payroll.</li><li>50+ employees → ADP or Paychex.</li></ul><h2><strong>3. Employer Accounts to Set Up</strong></h2><p>☐ Federal EIN (already required for banking).<br /> ☐ State employer tax account (for unemployment and withholding).<br /> ☐ Local employer tax account (if applicable).<br /> ☐ Workers’ compensation insurance policy.</p><h2><strong>4. Payroll Policies</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Pay Schedule:</strong> Weekly, biweekly, or semi-monthly. (Biweekly is standard.)</li><li><strong>Overtime Rules:</strong> Track hours &gt;40 per week (state rules may vary).</li><li><strong>Direct Deposit:</strong> Encourage all employees to use.</li><li><strong>Cutoff Times:</strong> Define when hours must be submitted for approval.</li></ul><h2><strong>5. Payroll Process Flow</strong></h2><ol><li>Collect employee hours (via timekeeping system).</li><li>Review and approve hours (supervisor/manager).</li><li>Submit payroll through payroll provider.</li><li>Provider calculates gross pay, deductions, employer taxes.</li><li>Direct deposit issued to employees.</li><li>Payroll provider remits taxes to IRS/state.</li><li>Accounting software posts journal entries.</li></ol><h2><strong>6. Compliance Requirements</strong></h2><p>☐ File quarterly federal tax returns (Form 941).<br /> ☐ File annual federal tax return (Form 940 for FUTA).<br /> ☐ Issue W-2s to employees (by Jan 31).<br /> ☐ Issue 1099s to contractors (by Jan 31).<br /> ☐ Maintain I-9 forms for all employees.<br /> ☐ Retain payroll records for 4+ years.</p><h2><strong>7. Best Practices</strong></h2><ul><li>Automate as much as possible (time tracking → payroll → bookkeeping).</li><li>Never mix employees and contractors in the same workflow — track separately.</li><li>Review payroll register before final submission each cycle.</li><li>Keep a payroll reserve account with at least 1 month of gross payroll.</li><li>Conduct annual payroll audit to confirm compliance with labor/tax laws.</li></ul><p>✅ <strong>Action Step:</strong> Pick your payroll provider, set up federal/state accounts, and establish your pay schedule + policies. Document everything in your Financial SOP.</p>$C$, 5, true),
    (mid, $S$Financial Policies & Controls$S$, $S$financial-policies-controls$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Purpose</strong></h3><p>To establish rules and procedures that govern how business money is handled, approved, and protected.</p><h2><strong>1. Spending &amp; Expense Policies</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Authorized Spending:</strong><ul><li>Only designated staff can make purchases.</li><li>Purchases &gt; $___ require written pre-approval.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Business Credit Cards:</strong><ul><li>Issued only to approved staff.</li><li>No personal purchases allowed.</li><li>Receipts must be submitted within 24 hours.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Cash Transactions:</strong><ul><li>Discouraged unless necessary.</li><li>Cash purchases require receipt + manager approval.</li></ul></li></ul><h2><strong>2. Approvals &amp; Separation of Duties</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Dual Control:</strong><ul><li>Payments &gt; $___ require two signatures/approvals.</li><li>Wire/ACH transfers require secondary verification.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Separation of Duties:</strong><ul><li>The person who approves purchases is not the same person reconciling books.</li><li>Owner/manager reviews monthly reconciliations.</li></ul></li></ul><h2><strong>3. Vendor &amp; Bill Payment Policies</strong></h2><ul><li>All new vendors require W-9 + approval before first payment.</li><li>Bills must be uploaded to accounting software before payment.</li><li>Use Bill.com or Melio for approval workflows (if available).</li><li>Late fees are not acceptable — bills must be paid on time.</li></ul><h2><strong>4. Revenue Handling Policies</strong></h2><ul><li>All payments must be deposited into business bank account within 24–48 hours.</li><li>No customer payments are deposited into personal accounts.</li><li>Credit card/ACH processors must be linked only to the business account.</li><li>Cash deposits require dual verification.</li></ul><h2><strong>5. Reserve &amp; Savings Policies</strong></h2><ul><li>Maintain at least <strong>3 months of operating expenses</strong> in a reserve account.</li><li>Transfer ___% of revenue into a tax reserve account weekly or biweekly.</li><li>Profit allocation (if applicable):<ul><li>___% to Owner’s Pay</li><li>___% to Operating Expenses</li><li>___% to Taxes</li><li>___% to Profit/Reserves (e.g., “Profit First” method)</li></ul></li></ul><h2><strong>6. Financial Reporting Policies</strong></h2><ul><li>Monthly financial statements (P&amp;L, Balance Sheet, Cash Flow) must be prepared by the 10th of each month.</li><li>Owner/manager reviews reports and signs off.</li><li>Budget vs. Actual report reviewed quarterly.</li><li>Annual financial review with CPA required.</li></ul><h2><strong>7. Fraud Prevention &amp; Security</strong></h2><ul><li>Enable alerts for all bank and credit card transactions.</li><li>Restrict online banking access to essential staff only.</li><li>Require 2FA for all financial accounts.</li><li>Conduct random spot-checks of expense receipts.</li><li>Immediately terminate access for departing employees.</li></ul><h2><strong>8. Recordkeeping &amp; Compliance</strong></h2><ul><li>Retain all financial records for 7 years (digital preferred).</li><li>Store financial data in encrypted/cloud-secure folders.</li><li>Comply with IRS, state, and local recordkeeping rules.</li></ul><h3><strong>Best Practices</strong></h3><ul><li>Put all policies in writing and have staff acknowledge receipt.</li><li>Start small (one approval level, one reviewer) and scale up as the team grows.</li><li>Build redundancy — at least two people know how to run payroll, reconcile books, and access bank accounts.</li></ul><p>✅ <strong>Action Step:</strong> Document your policies now, assign approval levels, and store this SOP with your financial infrastructure docs.</p>$C$, 6, true),
    (mid, $S$Financial Reporting & KPIs$S$, $S$financial-reporting-kpis$S$, $C$<h3><strong>Purpose</strong></h3><p>To establish a reporting system that gives business owners a clear, accurate picture of performance each month — and defines which numbers matter most (the KPIs).</p><h2><strong>1. Core Financial Reports</strong></h2><p>Every business should generate these <strong>monthly</strong> (at minimum):</p><ul><li><strong>Profit &amp; Loss Statement (P&amp;L)</strong><ul><li>Shows revenue, expenses, and net profit.</li><li>Answers: <em>“Am I profitable?”</em></li></ul></li><li><strong>Balance Sheet</strong><ul><li>Shows assets, liabilities, and equity.</li><li>Answers: <em>“What do I own vs. what do I owe?”</em></li></ul></li><li><strong>Cash Flow Statement</strong><ul><li>Shows actual cash movement in/out.</li><li>Answers: <em>“Do I have enough cash to run the business?”</em></li></ul></li></ul><p>✅ Best Practice: Review these by the <strong>10th of each month</strong> for the prior month.</p><h2><strong>2. Key Financial KPIs (track monthly)</strong></h2><h3><strong>Profitability</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Gross Margin %</strong> = (Revenue – COGS) ÷ Revenue</li><li><strong>Net Margin %</strong> = Net Profit ÷ Revenue</li></ul><h3><strong>Liquidity</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Current Ratio</strong> = Current Assets ÷ Current Liabilities<br /> (Healthy benchmark: &gt;1.5)</li><li><strong>Quick Ratio</strong> = (Cash + Receivables) ÷ Current Liabilities</li></ul><h3><strong>Efficiency</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Accounts Receivable Days</strong> = (A/R ÷ Revenue) × 30</li><li><strong>Accounts Payable Days</strong> = (A/P ÷ COGS) × 30</li></ul><h3><strong>Cash &amp; Reserves</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Cash Runway</strong> = Cash on Hand ÷ Monthly Expenses (in months)</li><li><strong>Operating Cash Flow</strong> = Cash from operations ÷ Total Revenue</li></ul><h3><strong>Growth</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Revenue Growth %</strong> = (Current Period Revenue – Prior Period Revenue) ÷ Prior Period</li><li><strong>Customer Acquisition Cost (CAC)</strong> (if marketing tracked)</li><li><strong>Customer Lifetime Value (LTV)</strong> (if recurring/subscription)</li></ul><h2><strong>3. Reporting SOP</strong></h2><ol><li><strong>By the 5th of each month</strong>: Reconcile accounts.</li><li><strong>By the 10th</strong>: Generate P&amp;L, Balance Sheet, Cash Flow.</li><li><strong>Owner/Manager reviews KPIs</strong> → Gross Margin, Net Margin, Cash Runway.</li><li><strong>Quarterly</strong>: Compare Budget vs. Actual; adjust forecasts.</li><li><strong>Annually</strong>: Full year financial review with CPA or CFO advisor.</li></ol><h2><strong>4. Best Practices</strong></h2><ul><li>Automate reporting via QuickBooks, Xero, or dashboard tools.</li><li>Don’t drown in data → pick <strong>3–5 KPIs</strong> that matter most for your business type.</li><li>Share reports with advisors, board members, or investors monthly.</li><li>Track trends, not just snapshots — is gross margin improving or declining?</li></ul><p>✅ <strong>Action Step:</strong> Set a monthly financial review cadence, decide your top 5 KPIs, and automate reporting where possible.</p><div class="lesson-template"><h3>📄 Template for this lesson</h3><p>Download and use the worksheet that goes with this lesson:</p><a class="dl" href="/templates/financial-advantage/3-year-instructions.docx" download="3-year Instructions.docx">⬇ Download How to Use the 3-Year Forecast Template</a></div>$C$, 7, true),
    (mid, $S$SOP: Business Banking$S$, $S$sop-business-banking$S$, $C$<p><strong>Purpose:</strong><br /> To ensure all business banking transactions are handled securely, consistently, and in compliance with financial best practices.</p><p><strong>Scope:</strong><br /> Applies to all owners, managers, and authorized staff handling company funds.</p><p><strong>Effective Date:</strong> ____________<br /> <strong>Approved By:</strong> ____________</p><h2><strong>1. Account Structure</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Primary Business Checking</strong> – Daily operations (income &amp; expenses).</li><li><strong>Business Savings</strong> – Taxes, reserves, reinvestment.</li><li><strong>Tax Account (Optional)</strong> – Dedicated for estimated quarterly/annual tax transfers.</li><li><strong>Business Credit Account(s)</strong> – Used for authorized business purchases only.</li></ul><h2><strong>2. Deposits</strong></h2><ul><li>All incoming payments must be deposited into the <strong>Business Checking Account</strong> only.</li><li><strong>Cash Deposits:</strong><ul><li>Counted and verified by two staff members (if applicable).</li><li>Deposit slip completed and copy filed.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Checks:</strong><ul><li>Endorsed with business stamp.</li><li>Deposited within 24–48 hours of receipt.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Electronic Payments:</strong><ul><li>Verify funds received via online banking.</li><li>Record transaction in bookkeeping software.</li></ul></li></ul><h2><strong>3. Withdrawals &amp; Payments</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Authorized Users Only</strong> may make withdrawals or payments.</li><li>Payments must be for <strong>business purposes only.</strong></li><li><strong>Credit Card Purchases:</strong><ul><li>Only pre-approved categories (e.g., supplies, travel, equipment).</li><li>Receipts uploaded to bookkeeping system within 24 hours.</li></ul></li><li><strong>ACH / Wire Transfers:</strong><ul><li>Require dual approval for amounts over $______.</li><li>Confirmation number recorded in transaction log.</li></ul></li></ul><h2><strong>4. Transfers Between Accounts</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Operating to Savings:</strong> Minimum ___% of each deposit transferred weekly to reserves.</li><li><strong>Operating to Tax Account:</strong> ___% of each deposit transferred automatically or bi-weekly.</li><li>All transfers documented in bookkeeping system.</li></ul><h2><strong>5. Bookkeeping &amp; Reconciliation</strong></h2><ul><li>Bank accounts must be synced with accounting software (QuickBooks, Xero, Wave, etc.).</li><li><strong>Weekly:</strong> Verify deposits and categorize expenses.</li><li><strong>Monthly:</strong> Reconcile bank statements to accounting records.</li><li><strong>Quarterly:</strong> Review reserves and adjust tax transfer percentage if needed.</li></ul><h2><strong>6. Security &amp; Controls</strong></h2><ul><li>Never co-mingle personal and business funds.</li><li>Change online banking passwords every 90 days.</li><li>Two-factor authentication required for all online banking logins.</li><li>Bank notifications/alerts enabled for withdrawals, deposits, and low balances.</li></ul><h2><strong>7. Business Banker Relationship</strong></h2><ul><li>Maintain ongoing communication with assigned <strong>Business Banker.</strong></li><li>Schedule annual review of accounts, credit, and loan opportunities.</li></ul><h2><strong>8. Compliance</strong></h2><ul><li>Retain all deposit slips, receipts, and bank statements for <strong>7 years.</strong></li><li>Follow IRS, state, and local guidelines for business financial records.</li><li>Ensure transactions comply with anti-money laundering (AML) and Know Your Customer (KYC) laws.</li></ul><p><strong>Review Schedule:</strong> This SOP must be reviewed <strong>annually</strong> or when major changes occur in banking processes.</p><div class="lesson-template"><h3>📄 Template for this lesson</h3><p>Download and use the worksheet that goes with this lesson:</p><a class="dl" href="/templates/financial-advantage/business-banking-setup-checklist.docx" download="Business Banking Setup Checklist.docx">⬇ Download Business Banking Setup Checklist</a></div>$C$, 8, true),
    (mid, $S$SOP: Bookkeeping$S$, $S$sop-bookkeeping$S$, $C$<p><strong>Purpose:</strong><br /> To ensure accurate, consistent tracking of all financial transactions so that business decisions, tax filings, and funding requests are based on reliable data.</p><p><strong>Scope:</strong><br /> Applies to all business transactions, including sales, expenses, payroll, and transfers.</p><p><strong>Effective Date:</strong> ____________<br /> <strong>Approved By:</strong> ____________</p><h2><strong>1. Daily Tasks</strong></h2><ul><li>Record all sales and income (cash, checks, card payments).</li><li>Save and upload receipts for any business purchases.</li><li>Send invoices for any billable work completed.</li><li>Review bank feeds to confirm transactions are syncing into accounting software.</li></ul><h2><strong>2. Weekly Tasks</strong></h2><ul><li>Categorize all transactions (income/expense) in accounting software.</li><li>Match deposits and payments against bank records.</li><li>Follow up on overdue invoices.</li><li>File copies of vendor bills (digital is fine).</li><li>Pay approved bills if due.</li></ul><h2><strong>3. Monthly Tasks</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Bank Reconciliation</strong>: Match your books to the official bank statement.</li><li>Review and adjust Chart of Accounts categories as needed.</li><li>Generate financial reports:<ul><li>Profit &amp; Loss (P&amp;L)</li><li>Balance Sheet</li><li>Cash Flow Statement</li></ul></li><li>Compare actual spending vs. budget.</li><li>Meet with bookkeeper/CPA for review (if applicable).</li></ul><h2><strong>4. Quarterly Tasks</strong></h2><ul><li>File quarterly estimated taxes (if required).</li><li>Review payroll tax filings (Form 941, state equivalents).</li><li>Review financial KPIs (e.g., gross margin, net profit %).</li><li>Adjust budget forecasts if business conditions have shifted.</li></ul><h2><strong>5. Annual Tasks</strong></h2><ul><li>Close out books for year-end (lock entries after CPA review).</li><li>Issue 1099s to contractors and W-2s to employees (by Jan 31).</li><li>Prepare for annual tax return (provide CPA with reconciled books + reports).</li><li>Archive all digital receipts, invoices, and statements for compliance (7-year retention).</li></ul><h2><strong>6. Controls &amp; Security</strong></h2><ul><li>Only authorized staff may access bookkeeping software.</li><li>All receipts and invoices must be uploaded digitally — no paper-only storage.</li><li>All changes to books must have an audit trail (who, when, what).</li><li>No personal expenses allowed in business accounts.</li></ul><h2><strong>7. Roles</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Owner/Manager:</strong> Ensures compliance and reviews monthly reports.</li><li><strong>Bookkeeper (in-house or outsourced):</strong> Handles daily/weekly/monthly data entry and reconciliations.</li><li><strong>Accountant/CPA:</strong> Oversees quarterly/annual reviews, tax planning, and compliance.</li></ul><div class="lesson-template"><h3>📄 Template for this lesson</h3><p>Download and use the worksheet that goes with this lesson:</p><a class="dl" href="/templates/financial-advantage/monthly-reconciliation-checklist.docx" download="Monthly Reconciliation Checklist.docx">⬇ Download Monthly Reconciliation Checklist</a></div>$C$, 9, true),
    (mid, $S$SOP: Payroll Processing$S$, $S$sop-payroll-processing$S$, $C$<p><strong>Purpose:</strong><br /> To ensure accurate, timely, and compliant payroll for all employees and contractors.</p><p><strong>Scope:</strong><br /> Applies to all staff, contractors, and payroll administrators.</p><p><strong>Effective Date:</strong> ____________<br /> <strong>Approved By:</strong> ____________</p><h2><strong>1. Pre-Payroll Setup</strong></h2><ul><li>Maintain updated employee files (W-4, I-9, state tax forms, direct deposit info).</li><li>Maintain contractor files (W-9 for all 1099 workers).</li><li>Confirm pay schedule (weekly/biweekly/semi-monthly).</li><li>Ensure timekeeping system is synced with payroll system.</li></ul><h2><strong>2. Payroll Cycle Process</strong></h2><p><strong>Step 1 – Collect Hours</strong><br /> ☐ Employees submit time by cutoff (via timesheets or time-tracking app).<br /> ☐ Supervisors review and approve hours.</p><p><strong>Step 2 – Review Data</strong><br /> ☐ Verify hourly/salary rates are correct.<br /> ☐ Confirm overtime hours (if &gt;40/week, or state-specific).<br /> ☐ Review deductions (benefits, garnishments, retirement).</p><p><strong>Step 3 – Submit Payroll</strong><br /> ☐ Log into payroll system (e.g., Gusto, ADP, QuickBooks Payroll).<br /> ☐ Enter or import hours and adjustments.<br /> ☐ Run preview report to confirm totals.</p><p><strong>Step 4 – Approval</strong><br /> ☐ Owner/Manager reviews and approves final payroll register.<br /> ☐ Ensure total payroll aligns with reserve account balance.</p><p><strong>Step 5 – Payment</strong><br /> ☐ Payroll provider issues direct deposits or checks.<br /> ☐ Payroll taxes automatically withheld and scheduled for remittance.</p><h2><strong>3. Post-Payroll Tasks</strong></h2><p>☐ Save payroll register and reports in digital payroll folder.<br /> ☐ Record payroll journal entry into accounting software:</p><ul><li>Debit: Payroll Expenses (wages, taxes, benefits).</li><li>Credit: Cash, Payroll Liabilities.<br /> ☐ Verify funds withdrawn from bank match payroll totals.<br /> ☐ Update payroll reserve account (replenish if used).</li></ul><h2><strong>4. Contractor Payments (1099)</strong></h2><p>☐ Pay contractors separately from payroll system (ACH, check, or expense tool).<br /> ☐ Track total annual payments per contractor.<br /> ☐ Issue 1099-NEC forms by Jan 31 for all &gt;$600.</p><h2><strong>5. Compliance &amp; Filings</strong></h2><p>☐ File IRS Form 941 quarterly (federal withholding &amp; payroll taxes).<br /> ☐ File IRS Form 940 annually (FUTA).<br /> ☐ Submit state/local payroll filings (frequency varies).<br /> ☐ Issue W-2s to employees by Jan 31.<br /> ☐ Retain all payroll records for 4 years minimum.</p><h2><strong>6. Security &amp; Controls</strong></h2><ul><li>Only payroll administrator + owner have system access.</li><li>Require two-factor authentication for payroll software.</li><li>Prohibit shared logins; each user must have unique credentials.</li><li>Conduct annual payroll audit (verify pay rates, tax filings, compliance).</li></ul>$C$, 10, true);
